*   The only alkaline agent mentioned is NaOH for pH adjustment of the ethanol production medium and xanthan gum fermentation medium.\n    *   The question specifies "exclude pH adjustment".\n    *   Therefore, the statement is False.\n\n5.  **Final Conclusion:** The methodology does not explicitly describe the use of alkaline pretreatment on the substrate (PCR) using an alkaline agent. The substrate was subjected to organosolv pretreatment (ethanol + H2SO4). The alkaline agent (NaOH) was only used for pH adjustment in the fermentation medium, not for substrate pretreatment for anaerobic digestion.\n\n    *   Wait, the question says "Pretreatment should be understood as increasing bioavailability... by some treatment before the incorporation in the digestion."\n    *   The study\'s substrate was PCR. It was pretreated with ethanol (organosolv).\n    *   Then it was hydrolyzed with enzymes.\n    *   Then it was digested.\n    *   The alkaline agent (NaOH) was added to the medium (2.4) for pH 5 (ethanol fermentation).\n    *   There is no mention of NaOH or other base being added to the PCR before digestion for pretreatment purposes.\n    *   So, False.\n\n    Wait, I need to check if "alkaline agent" refers to the organosolv process itself. Organosolv typically uses organics (alcohol). Ethanol is an alcohol. H2SO4 is an acid catalyst.\n    There is no alkaline agent mentioned for the substrate pretreatment.\n    So the answer is False.\n\n    Let\'s verify the text again.\n    Section 2.2: "180 g of organosolv solution... 50 or 75% vv^-1 ethanol solution... 1% ww^-1 H2SO4".\n    Section 2.4: "pH was set to 5 with the addition of the NaOH solution (2 M)".\n    Section 2.6: "No additional nutrient was required".\n\n    The question asks: "Does the methodology explicitly describe the use of alkaline pretreatment on a substrate, by the addition of a alkaline agent."\n    The text describes "ethanolic organosolv pretreatment" with ethanol and H2SO4. It does not describe a