rgy consumption and technical data related to mechanical pretreatments."\n    -   There is no section describing the addition of external organic material (compost, sludge, etc.) as a pretreatment step.\n    -   The study focuses on the mechanical pretreatments (KM, HM, EX, SHC) applied to agri-based feedstocks (maize silage, manure, seeds, etc.) directly.\n    -   Therefore, the methodology does not explicitly describe using external active organic material (like compost, digestate, sludge) as a pretreatment step.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nFalse. The methodology focuses on mechanical pretreatments (physical) and does not describe the use of external active organic material (like compost, sludge, digestate) as a pretreatment step.\n\n**Double Check:**\n-   Question: "Excluding inoculum or other sludge necessary for starting the anaerobical biological activity in the digester, does the methodology explicitly describe the use of a external active organic material, such as but not only: rumen, compost, digestate, aerobic sludge or similar, as a pretreatment step; specifically to degrade or modify the substrate before anaerobic digestion, with the explicit purpose of increasing its bioavailability?"\n-   The text says "Pretreatment methods can be typically classified into physical, chemical, physico-chemical and biological." Then it says "Among physical pretreatments, mechanical pretreatments...".\n-   The study uses "agri-based feedstock... samples taken from four different full-scale biogas plants... operating with such mechanical pretreatment."\n-   The methods section describes mechanical pretreatments (knife milling, hammer milling, extrusion, shredding + hydrodynamic cavitation).\n-   There is no mention of adding compost, digestate, or other sludge as a pretreatment step in the provided text.\n-   The study is about evaluating mechanical pretreatments on agri-based feedstocks directly.\n-   Therefore, the methodology does not explicitly describe the use of external act